{"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "section_title", "parent_tag": "0083", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\section{", "suffix": "}\n\\label{section-sheafify-abelian-presheaves}"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:b6b755f43b68f05ba4f231ed787381cd693a2eabfb1b3b7cacf319280e79f192", "source_text": "Sheafification of abelian presheaves", "source_text_hash": "sha256:5db4e2e56e4bbc39084f076506ba5d9681af888a18df7e7e28f97a5fd3729406", "unit_id": "tag:0083:title"}
{"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0083", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:d4660e1cd3dcfca8dba79ae8ed2280634eaf1694f08114a326d0131d037ab83b", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nThe following strange looking lemma is likely unnecessary, but\nvery convenient to deal with sheafification of presheaves\nof algebraic structures.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:89704cdb45264e78a474748b7b03b8eab92eee590d0d79d7ebe4673c23d3c2ff", "unit_id": "tag:0083:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "0083", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$X$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0003": "$X$", "MATH_0004": "$U \\subset X$", "MATH_0005": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\n\\mathcal{F}^\\#(U) \\ar[d] \\ar[r] &\n\\Pi(\\mathcal{F})(U) \\ar[d] \\\\\n\\prod_{x \\in U} \\mathcal{F}_x\n\\ar[r] &\n\\prod_{x \\in U} \\Pi(\\mathcal{F})_x\n}\n$$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{F}^\\#(U) \\to \\mathcal{F}^\\#_x = \\mathcal{F}_x$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{F} \\to \\Pi(\\mathcal{F})$", "MATH_0008": "$\\Pi(\\mathcal{F})(U) \\to \\Pi(\\mathcal{F})_x$", "MATH_0009": "$\\mathcal{F}_x \\to \\Pi(\\mathcal{F})_x$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathcal{F} \\to \\Pi(\\mathcal{F})$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-stalk-sheafification}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{section-sheafification}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{section-sheafification}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{enumerate}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\item", "STRUCT_0003": "\\item", "STRUCT_0004": "\\item", "STRUCT_0005": "\\item", "STRUCT_0006": "\\end{enumerate}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-diagram-fibre-product}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:2d4bafe1f78115be4c0ac5c82919a2ad1c61f80a8d2952ab1b2af9914cb9e6bc", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:ed435e0239951a349cd136e20ddf3fe44e5d82d752d427272aab28d6f1e1b7bc", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a topological space. Let <MATH_0002> be\na presheaf of sets on <MATH_0003>. Let <MATH_0004> be open.\nThere is a canonical fibre product diagram\n<MATH_0005>\nwhere the maps are the following:\n<STRUCT_0001>\n<STRUCT_0002> The left vertical map has components\n<MATH_0006>\nwhere the equality is Lemma <REF_0001>.\n<STRUCT_0003> The top horizontal map comes from the\nmap of presheaves <MATH_0007> described\nin Section <REF_0002>.\n<STRUCT_0004> The right vertical map has obvious component\nmaps <MATH_0008>.\n<STRUCT_0005> The bottom horizontal map has components\n<MATH_0009>\nwhich come from the map of presheaves\n<MATH_0010> described\nin Section <REF_0003>.\n<STRUCT_0006>\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:246e1d7dfb93a2c88a3f4680d96986d731c58f53b831c41ed08fc8bab7498a81", "unit_id": "tag:0084:statement"}
{"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "0083", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{F}_x \\to \\Pi(\\mathcal{F})_x$", "MATH_0002": "$s \\in \\Pi(\\mathcal{F})(U)$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{F}^\\#$", "MATH_0004": "$(*)$", "MATH_0005": "$(*)$", "MATH_0006": "$s$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0008": "$s$", "MATH_0009": "$\\Pi(\\mathcal{F})_x$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathcal{F}_x$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-stalk-sheafification}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:ce60cedc0766cb513580f8fb8cd9ee5786a54dc7acf8a6b5a240ef45ca00adea", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:be75e12d9fd7cbcfc867206700782740259b7f953d6cd767d411276004a13021", "source_text": "It is clear that the diagram commutes. We have to show\nit is a fibre product diagram. The bottom horizontal arrow\nis injective since all the maps <MATH_0001>\nare injective (see beginning proof of\nLemma <REF_0001>).\nA section <MATH_0002> is in <MATH_0003> if and\nonly if <MATH_0004> holds. But <MATH_0005> says that around every point\nthe section <MATH_0006> comes from a section of <MATH_0007>. By definition\nof the stalk functors, this is equivalent to saying that\nthe value of <MATH_0008> in every stalk <MATH_0009> comes\nfrom an element of the stalk <MATH_0010>. Hence the lemma.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f5364ac65183d397caec55ae06939a557f4dc9f2a5377805236418411cbabb83", "unit_id": "tag:0084:proof"}
